<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saco, Maine</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saco is a city in southern Maine, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, on the Saco River near its mouth, opposite Biddeford. English fishermen and settlers arrived in the 1620s and 1630s, and it formed part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinando-gorges">Sir Ferdinando Gorges</a>'s Province of Maine before Massachusetts absorbed the region in the 1650s. In the 19th century it grew as a textile mill town.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saco-maine.jpg" alt="Saco, Maine" width="320" height="228" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kenneth C. Zirkel · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Main_Street,_Saco_Maine.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Saco lies beside Saco Bay on the Gulf of Maine in York County and is drained by the Saco River. It borders Biddeford, its twin city, and the towns of Scarborough, Buxton, Dayton and Old Orchard Beach. Its landforms include beaches, fields, forests, bogs and urban areas.</p><p>The beachfront Camp Ellis section has been eroding since the Army Corps of Engineers built a jetty in the 1860s at the mouth of the Saco River, which stopped sediment from feeding the beaches there. The territory was that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abenaki">Abenaki</a>, whose fortified village stood up the Sokokis Trail at Pequawket, now Fryeburg.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A settlement with homes and permanent cultivation stood at the mouth of the river when Europeans arrived in the early 1600s. In July 1607, 500 warriors led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikmaq">Mi'kmaq</a> sakmow Henri Membertou attacked the village, and the conflict lasted until 1615. In 1630 the Plymouth Company granted Thomas Lewis and Richard Bonython a charter for a town at Saco, settled in 1631. The government of Maine under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinando-gorges">Ferdinando Gorges</a> was based there from 1636 to 1653, when the Massachusetts General Court reorganised the place as Saco.</p><p>The settlement was attacked in 1675 during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-philips-war">King Philip's War</a>, and again in 1689 and in 1723 during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dummers-war">Dummer's War</a>, since it lay in territory contested between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a>. The community was incorporated as Biddeford in 1718. In 1762 the northeastern bank separated as the District of Pepperrellborough, named for Sir William Pepperrell, and became a town in 1775. Residents found the name cumbersome and in 1805 renamed it Saco. It became a city in 1867.</p><p>Saco was a lumbering centre, with 17 sawmills at Saco Falls producing 21,000,000 feet of sawn lumber in 1827. The Saco Iron Works began on Factory Island in 1811, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> mill was founded in 1826. After the Portland, Saco and Portsmouth Railroad arrived in 1842, Factory Island became a major textile centre. The York Manufacturing Company closed in 1958.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The population was 20,381 at the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Saco belongs to the Portland-South Portland-Biddeford metropolitan area, and Amtrak's Downeaster connects it with Portland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>. The mill era left fine Georgian, Federal, Greek Revival and Victorian buildings, many listed on the National Register of Historic Places. Laurel Hill Cemetery, established in 1844, is one of the earliest examples of the Rural cemetery movement.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saco, Maine” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Fishing stations and small settlements grew along the coast at Kittery, York and <b>Saco</b>, and in 1639 a royal charter made Gorges proprietor of the Province of Maine.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-maine">Province of Maine</a></li></ul></section>
